(function () {
  if (typeof BRANDS_DATA === "undefined") return;
  var EN = {
    "San Cristobal": {
      founded: "2007",
      founder: "Published by Ashton Distributors of the United States (founded by Robert G. Levin); manufactured by the family of José \"Pepín\" García (My Father Cigars)",
      meaning: "Derived from \"San Cristóbal de la Habana\" (the Havana of Saint Christopher), the formal name of the Cuban capital. San Cristóbal is Havana's patron saint, and a cigar marque of the same name is said to have existed in early twentieth-century Cuba",
      status: "A Nicaraguan brand marketed by Ashton Distributors and manufactured at the García family's My Father Cigars S.A. in Estelí. Alongside the original it comprises the Revelation, Quintessence, Ovation and Elegancia lines, and is a regular presence in Cigar Aficionado's Top 25",
      history: "【The Origin of the Name】In 1514 Diego Velázquez de Cuéllar of Spain is said to have founded a settlement in present-day Cuba that was named \"San Cristóbal de la Habana\" — the Havana of Saint Christopher. San Cristóbal (Saint Christopher) is the patron saint of travellers and ferrymen, and was also the patron saint of the city. This was the formal, stately name of the place later abbreviated simply to \"Havana\". The city that bore the name of the protector of travellers would in time become the cigar capital of the world, and its name would be inherited by a succession of illustrious marques. A cigar brand of the same name existed in Cuba in the early twentieth century and is said to have had a history of more than a hundred years, but it reportedly died out after the Castro revolution of 1959. Its trademark was secured in the 1980s by the Levin family of the United States, who are said to have bided their time awaiting an opportunity for revival. More than two decades later, in 2007, this storied name was reborn as a Nicaraguan cigar. From the outset, therefore, the name of the present-day San Cristobal has carried deeply inscribed within it a yearning for the lost cigar culture of Cuba.\n【Ashton, the Publisher】Its publisher, Ashton Distributors, was founded by Robert G. Levin, who grew up in Holt's Cigar Company, a venerable Philadelphia tobacconist. Levin learned the cigar trade in the 1950s and 1960s at Holt's, the shop his father ran, took over the store in the 1970s, and in the early 1980s conceived his own brand, \"Ashton\", which he launched in 1985 in partnership with the Fuente family of the Dominican Republic. Ashton is said to have remained among the \"top three\" brands in the United States for more than thirty years, and the company also owns marques such as La Aroma de Cuba as well as the Savoy range of humidors. Today it is run as a father-and-son business by Robert and his son Sathya Levin, and its products reach more than sixty countries. This \"publisher-like\" way of doing business — rolling no cigars itself, but bringing great marques into the world in partnership with outstanding makers — laid the groundwork for the later birth of San Cristobal.\n【A House Handed from Father to Son】The conviction Ashton has consistently upheld is that cigars are best suited to family firms. The Levin family, who built the ancestral business at Holt's in Philadelphia; the Fuente family, who roll Ashton in the Dominican Republic; and the García family, who roll San Cristobal and La Aroma de Cuba in Nicaragua — every strand of the Ashton story is woven from the craft and trust of families spanning several generations. As management passes from Robert Levin to his son Sathya, San Cristobal too is being handed on to the next generation, in step with the generational transition of its makers, the Garcías. That the partnership of three families — the Levins as publisher, the Fuentes in the Dominican Republic and the Garcías in Nicaragua — has in each case endured across generations is in itself a rare form of stability in this industry. The fact that both publisher and maker are family businesses underpins, at the brand's foundation, its unhurried character untroubled by passing fashion and its consistent long-term quality. Cultivating relationships rather than chasing numbers — the Ashton way lives on in San Cristobal.\n【The Encounter with Pepín】In the mid-2000s, as Nicaraguan cigars swept the market with their powerful flavours, Ashton resolved to create its first Nicaraguan brand. The man it chose to entrust with production was José \"Pepín\" García, the exiled Cuban roller then coming to prominence at the Miami workshop El Rey de los Habanos. For Pepín, who had already rolled a string of acclaimed marques such as Tatuaje and was regarded in the trade as its next leading figure, collaboration with a publisher of Ashton's standing was a golden opportunity to raise his reputation further. The pairing of a distinguished publisher with the maker widely held to be the finest of his day heightened expectations in the trade even before the announcement. San Cristobal was thus conceived as the product of a collaboration binding together the craft and trust of two families: the Levin publishing house, responsible for planning and sales, and the García family, responsible for manufacture. As it happened, the Garcías would launch their own brand \"My Father\" in 2008, immediately afterwards, and go on to great things; San Cristobal was born in the early stages of that ascent, a marque symbolising, as it were, the bond between the two houses. That the collaboration has continued unshaken even as the Garcías grew into a maker of world rank speaks of the depth of trust built between the families.\n【The Birth of the Original】In 2007 San Cristobal made its debut in style with five sizes at that year's RTDA (now PCA) trade show. A full-flavoured cigar with well-aged Nicaraguan long filler beneath a dark, oily wrapper, it is characterised by layered notes of dark chocolate, black pepper, espresso, black cherry and walnut. The balanced smoke typical of Pepín's cigars — powerful yet never heavy — was on full display here. As to the original's composition, accounts differ in detail: some describe it as a Nicaraguan puro, others as carrying an Ecuadorian Habano wrapper. In any event, its accomplishment was recognised early: it earned 93 points from Cigar Aficionado and later appeared several times in the magazine's Top 25, making Ashton's Nicaraguan venture an auspicious departure. The craft of the exiled roller Pepín had produced a cigar worthy of the Philadelphia house's name.\n【Selección del Sol】In 2009 came the first extension, \"Selección del Sol\" (selection of the sun). As the name suggests, it is a Nicaraguan puro wrapped in sun-grown leaf ripened in full sunlight, a medium-bodied cigar with notes of cocoa, orange peel and flowers that earned 92 points from the magazine. Exploring a brighter, more open register somewhat removed from the original's brooding power, it was the first step in growing San Cristobal from a single marque into a series with several faces. The method by which later San Cristobals would delineate their varied personalities by changing wrapper was already germinating in this second release.\n【Revelation】In 2013 the brand's third line, \"Revelation\", was announced. A fragrant, well-balanced medium-bodied blend clothed in an Ecuadorian Sumatra wrapper, it presents a refined middle way distinct from both the original's weight and Selección del Sol's brightness. True to its name, it also opened up a new facet of San Cristobal beyond sheer strength. The following year, 2014, its \"Legend\" size was named to Cigar Aficionado's Top 25 Cigars of the Year, confirming that San Cristobal was no fleeting success but a brand that generated high praise year after year. The collaboration between the publisher Ashton and the García makers was here steadily beginning to bear fruit.\n【Quintessence】\"Quintessence\", which appeared in 2016, is a medium-full cigar of Ecuadorian Habano wrapper and well-aged Nicaraguan long filler, and its accomplishment lived up to a name meaning the purest essence. Its Corona Gorda entered the Top 25 in 2018, and in 2021 the Churchill was ranked the magazine's No. 3 cigar of the year with 95 points; several sizes were thus honoured repeatedly. A decade and more after the original, the brand here marked a point of arrival. That the family which twice took the year's No. 1 spot with its own My Father brand should, in the same period, drive this marque rolled for the publisher Ashton nearly to the summit corroborates, from another angle, the maturity of the Garcías' manufacturing power. It is a cigar in which the maker's ripeness and Ashton's skill in nurturing marques crystallised together. With its refined complexity, distinct from the original's gravity, it demonstrated afresh how far San Cristobal's expressive range had widened.\n【Ovation and Elegancia】The range broadened further. \"Ovation\" is a medium-full line in which a dusky Mexican San Andrés wrapper weaves together peppery spice, dark chocolate, charred wood and the sweetness of dried fruit — a marque of commanding presence worthy of a name meaning acclaim. By contrast, \"Elegancia\", dressed in Ecuadorian Connecticut Shade leaf, is a gentle, nutty medium-bodied smoke suited to devotees of lighter flavours and to those newly acquainted with cigars. Graceful and smooth as its name implies, it broadens the brand's appeal within a portfolio dominated by powerful cigars. From the weight of the original to the lightness of Elegancia, San Cristobal thus grew, under one publisher and one maker, into a comprehensive family covering a wide span of strength and flavour. That marques of such clearly differentiated character can stand side by side within a single brand is possible only through the maker's skill.\n【Flavours Drawn by the Wrapper】Surveying the San Cristobal lines, one sees that their distinct personalities are guided chiefly by the choice of wrapper leaf. The original's dark oily leaf, Selección del Sol's sun-grown, Revelation's Ecuadorian Sumatra, Quintessence's Ecuadorian Habano, Ovation's Mexican San Andrés and Elegancia's Ecuadorian Connecticut Shade — upon the same foundation of the Garcías' Nicaraguan leaf, the single covering leaf is changed to adjust deftly the proportions of strength and sweetness, spice and delicacy. To array so varied a set of wrapper expressions within a single brand is possible only when the maker's skill and the publisher's design mesh together. Keeping the quality of the underlying filler and binder constant while transforming the whole impression through the outer leaf — this is a feat peculiar to the Garcías, who know the nature of the leaf through and through. The devotee may choose, from within one family of marques, the cigar best suited to the strength and mood of the day.\n【Within the Ashton Domain】San Cristobal occupies a place of its own within the Ashton Distributors portfolio. The division of labour — the flagship Ashton lines rolled by the Fuentes of the Dominican Republic, the Cuban-styled La Aroma de Cuba and the Nicaraguan San Cristobal by the Garcías — mirrors Ashton's business philosophy of matching each marque with the maker best suited to it. Within the Levin family's publishing business, which owns neither fields nor factories but assembles fine cigars through discernment and trust, the standard-bearer of Nicaraguan strength is this San Cristobal. The demarcation whereby the Fuentes' Ashton carries Dominican refinement and the Garcías' San Cristobal Nicaraguan power gives the Ashton range a lucid depth. The brand stands at the point where the judgement of a venerable Philadelphia house crosses the craft of the artisans of Estelí.\n【Heirs to the Memory of Cuba】The memory of Cuba clings to the San Cristobal marque in two senses. One is the name itself, freighted with longing for the lost cigar culture of Havana. The other is the fact that the Garcías who actually roll the cigar are exiles who once rolled Montecristo and Cohiba in Cuba's state factories. Though the embargo denies them Cuban leaf itself, with Cuban-trained craft and Nicaraguan soil so like home the remembered taste of former days can be revived — San Cristobal is precisely the product of that conviction. \"La Aroma de Cuba\", also owned by the publisher Ashton, shares the same theme, and the endeavour to reconstruct the lost island's cigars on neighbouring soil forms one of the grand narratives running through the modern premium cigar, common to Padrón, My Father and many other exiled Cuban makers. San Cristobal is among the clearest embodiments of that narrative, in both its brand name and the origins of its makers.\n【An Established Reputation】Since the original's launch, several San Cristobal lines — Revelation (2014), Quintessence (2018 and 2021) — have repeatedly appeared in Cigar Aficionado's Top 25, establishing the brand's standing not as a passing sensation but as a consistent generator of high praise. The original itself is said to have been selected for the magazine's Top 25 more than once, confirming that the collaboration of publisher and maker has functioned over the long term. Rather than courting attention with a stream of new releases, the brand tends a small number of lines with care, polishing their accomplishment year upon year — a steady gait that faithfully reflects the character of the veteran publisher Ashton, which has held its \"top three\" position for more than thirty years. Modest in flash, but unfailingly returning satisfaction above a certain standard whichever marque one takes up — that unshakeable reliability is the true asset of the San Cristobal brand.\n【The Present Day】San Cristobal has settled in the market as a Nicaraguan brand in which the publisher Ashton's powers of planning and brand-building and the manufacturing strength of the Garcías — that is, of My Father — are joined, and it remains a fixture of Cigar Aficionado's Top 25. The family of marques running from the original through Selección del Sol, Revelation, Quintessence, Ovation and Elegancia now forms part of the fabric of the modern Nicaraguan cigar. It stands, too, as one of the industry's finest examples of how a publisher that rolls no cigars can nurture a great brand by joining hands with the foremost maker of the age. Bearing the name of Havana's patron saint and the city's old name, it quietly but surely reflects the great theme shared by the exiled Cuban makers: the revival of the memory of Cuba's lost cigar culture upon the volcanic soil of neighbouring Nicaragua. Like Padrón, Joya and My Father, San Cristobal too stands where the memory of Cuba meets the Nicaraguan earth, and the story of its collaboration continues to this day.",
      vitolas: [
        "San Cristobal (the original; launched 2007 in five sizes, 93 points)",
        "San Cristobal Selección del Sol (2009; sun-grown wrapper)",
        "San Cristobal Revelation Legend (launched 2013; Top 25 in 2014)",
        "San Cristobal Revelation (Ecuadorian Sumatra wrapper)",
        "San Cristobal Quintessence Churchill (No. 3 cigar of 2021, 95 points)",
        "San Cristobal Quintessence (Ecuadorian Habano wrapper)",
        "San Cristobal Ovation (medium-full, San Andrés wrapper)",
        "San Cristobal Elegancia (Ecuadorian Connecticut, mild to medium)"
      ],
      trivia: "【The Patron Saint's Name】The brand name derives from \"San Cristóbal de la Habana\", the formal name of the city of Havana, founded in 1514. San Cristóbal (Saint Christopher) is the city's patron saint; a cigar marque of the same name existed in early twentieth-century Cuba but died out after the revolution, and its trademark is said to have been secured in the 1980s by the publishing Levin family, who revived it.\n【Publisher and Maker】San Cristobal is a collaborative brand planned and sold by Ashton Distributors and manufactured in Estelí by My Father Cigars of the family of José Pepín García. The marque's history therefore stands at the crossing of two family histories: a venerable Philadelphia publisher and Cuban-exile makers."
    },
    "Rocky Patel": {
      founded: "1995 (established as the Indian Tabac Cigar Co.; market debut at the 1996 RTDA. Renamed Rocky Patel Premium Cigars in 2003)",
      founder: "Rakesh \"Rocky\" Patel (born 1961 in Bombay, now Mumbai, India; emigrated to the United States at fourteen, settling in Green Bay, Wisconsin). A former Hollywood entertainment lawyer",
      meaning: "Bears the name of founder Rocky Patel. The former name Indian Tabac was licensed from the Indian Motorcycle company and is said to have no direct connection with Patel's own Indian origins",
      status: "Independent. Owns the El Paraíso factory in Danlí, Honduras, and the TAVICUSA (Tabacalera Villa Cuba) factory in Estelí, Nicaragua. Headquartered in Naples, Florida. Own and contracted production together are said to total roughly 80,000 cigars a day",
      history: "【From Mumbai to Green Bay】\nThe story of Rocky Patel begins far from any cigar country, on a road linking India with the American Midwest. Founder Rakesh \"Rocky\" Patel was born in 1961 in what is now Mumbai (then Bombay), the son of a family that ran a ball-bearing factory. At fourteen he emigrated with his family to the United States, settling in Green Bay, Wisconsin. In starting from surroundings with no ties to tobacco culture or the cigar industry, his career is strikingly unusual in the cigar world. The path he cut for himself from nothing as an immigrant's son would later come to intertwine with the \"American dream\" narrative the brand projects.\nThe journey of an immigrant boy who had to acquire language and culture from scratch, and who went on to build one of America's foremost cigar brands, became the very origin of the rags-to-riches story the brand would later proclaim.\n【The Hollywood Lawyer】\nPatel took his bachelor's degree at the University of Wisconsin and his law degree at Southwestern Law School in Los Angeles, and made his way in Hollywood as an entertainment lawyer. A socially and financially successful attorney with film-industry notables among his clients — such was the man before he gave his life to cigars. The world of courtrooms and contracts, and the world of tobacco leaf and fermentation: Patel would bridge, in a single lifetime, two realms as far apart as could be. His choice to abandon a secure profession and plunge into an unknown industry must have struck those around him as reckless.\n\n【Film Sets and Cigars】\nHis awakening to cigars came in the early 1990s on film sets, smoking with actors during the waits between takes. The smokes he shared with the stars of the screen drew him into the deep world of the cigar. His passion was further deepened by his visits to the Grand Havana Room, the members' club in Beverly Hills frequented by celebrities such as Schwarzenegger and Harrison Ford. Curiously, this was the very club where Pete Johnson of Tatuaje worked and discovered cigars. Amid days surrounded by fine cigars, enthusiasm turned in time to professional ambition, and Patel resolved to relinquish his legal practice and throw himself into the cigar industry.\nIn the process by which a pastime begun in smokes shared with screen stars became the driving force behind a major enterprise, the sharp instincts of a former lawyer with an eye for opportunity were at work.\n【The Founding of Indian Tabac】\nIn 1995 Patel established the Indian Tabac Cigar Co. The following year, 1996, it made its formal debut at the RTDA (as it then was), the industry's largest trade show, held in Cincinnati, Ohio. The \"Indian\" of the name, though often misunderstood, is said to derive not from Patel's own Indian origins but from the celebrated Indian Motorcycle company, whose name he borrowed in return for a royalty. America was then in the midst of the 1990s cigar boom, and the Honduran-made Indian Tabac sold as fast as it could be shipped. To meet demand, Patel sent product to market without pause.\nAnswering the boom's voracious demand, Patel shipped product without stint and, through energetic rounds of the retail shops, raised the brand's profile in short order.\n【The Bust and the Turn to Quality】\nWhen the cigar boom passed, however, the market turned; many young brands, plagued by oversupply and uneven quality, were winnowed out. Patel too struggled to sustain inventory and reputation. He travelled ceaselessly among retailers across the country, pleading his products' case in person, striving to establish Indian Tabac as a premium brand that would survive beyond the boom's end. These patient itinerant days prompted the shift in his thinking towards competing on quality and trust rather than volume, laying the ground for the leap to come. In his turning of adversity into a forge for brand philosophy one glimpses the strategic nerve befitting a former lawyer.\nSurviving the shake-out etched deep into Patel the conviction that in a volatile market, quality and trust are the only true assets.\n【2003: His Own Name on the Band】\nIn 2003 Patel transformed his brand strategy. He renamed the company from Indian Tabac to Rocky Patel Premium Cigars, resolving to put forward not an anonymous brand but himself, the man. Keeping Indian Tabac as a value brand for catalogue and online sale (the brand was discontinued in 2014), he introduced for the retail trade a new series bearing his own name: \"Vintage\". To set aside an existing customer base and stake everything on his own name was, for its time, a considerable gamble. Yet this very step became the point from which the name \"Rocky Patel\" would rise to worldwide standing.\n\n【The Vintage Series】\nThe Vintage Series that became the flagship is distinguished above all by carrying the ageing year in each blend's name. The first, \"Vintage 1990\", wore a Honduran broadleaf maduro wrapper and became the memorable first cigar to bear the Rocky Patel name. The succeeding \"Vintage 1992\" used a ten-year-aged Ecuadorian Sumatra wrapper and became the standard-bearer loved as a staple for many years thereafter. \"1999 (Connecticut)\", \"2003\" and \"2006\" then joined to complete a set of five vintages. The plain clarity of numbers directly denoting the ageing year effectively impressed upon the market a brand image of patiently matured, serious cigars. The 1990 was twice named to Cigar Aficionado's Top 25, in 2004 and 2006, and the 1992 placed eighth in 2015, accumulating high critical honours.\nThe idea of crowning a cigar with its vintage year was ground-breaking in placing \"ageing\" — a virtue until then invoked only vaguely — at the centre of the product in a form plain to every eye.\n【The Edge】\n\"The Edge\", which appeared in 2004, came into the world on a novel premise: one hundred band-less cigars packed in a rough wooden box for sale. At first there were only two blends, Corojo and Maduro, in only two sizes, and retailers had to buy by the hundred-count wooden chest. The clean-cut design, stripped of ornament and staking everything on the power of the cigar itself, together with the bold, concentrated flavour born of its thick formats, won the hearts of full-bodied devotees. The Edge later expanded into Candela, Connecticut, Habano, Sumatra and other varied wrappers, growing into one of Rocky Patel's principal lines. The Habano was ranked seventh (94 points) in Cigar Aficionado's Top 25 of 2015.\nThe idea of removing the band and stripping away ornament to let the cigar itself do the talking was refreshing in a market then given to lavish packaging, and well displays Patel's experimental spirit.\n【Decade】\nIn 2009 Patel marked ten years since his entry into the cigar trade with \"Decade\". Combining an Ecuadorian Sumatra wrapper with a Mexican binder and fillers from Jamastrán in Honduras and from Panama, this cigar was first made at the El Paraíso factory in Danlí, Honduras. Its poised, graceful flavour, with a sweetness recalling black cherry and a nutty finish, was highly praised, earning 95 points from Cigar Aficionado and a place in the Top 25. In outgrowing the frame of a commemorative anniversary brand to become a permanent fixture kept in continuous production, Decade too counts among Patel's signature works.\n\n【Fifteenth Anniversary】\nIn 2010 came the \"Fifteenth Anniversary\", marking fifteen years since the founding. This medium-to-full cigar, which Patel himself described as \"the Decade on steroids\", joins high-primed Ecuadorian Habano wrapper to Nicaraguan binder and filler, the long leaves bunched in the Cuban \"entubado\" manner, each rolled singly into a tube. With its careful finish, triple cap included, its accomplishment was high, and it was selected for Cigar Aficionado's Top 25 no fewer than four times — in 2010, 2011, 2013 and 2018. For a single marque to break into the upper ranks so repeatedly is exceptional, and it stands as a cigar emblematic of the brand's powers.\nFour Top 25 selections for a single marque are all but unheard of, and they proved that Rocky Patel was no passing sensation but a brand equipped to supply high quality on a sustained basis.\n【A Factory Empire: El Paraíso and TAVICUSA】\nRocky Patel insisted strongly on owning factories rather than entrusting production to others. Besides \"El Paraíso\" in Danlí, Honduras, he maintains \"TAVICUSA\" (Tabacalera Villa Cuba S.A.) in Estelí, Nicaragua. TAVICUSA handles precise, boutique-scale production, turning out roughly 26,000 to 28,000 cigars a day, or close to seven million a year. Total output, own and contracted together, is said to approach 80,000 a day. His brother Nish Patel, his cousin Nimish Desai and the celebrated Cuban-born craftsman Hamlet Paredes serve as faces of the brand and sustain its family management. The head office is in Naples, Florida, and the vertically integrated production system reconciles the brand's quality with its scale.\nVertical integration, from field to rolling table to the world's shop shelves, restrains variation in quality and at the same time provides the base from which elaborate limited editions, such as the anniversary releases, can be mounted at speed.\n【Years of Glory: Sun Grown and the Anniversaries】\nEmblematic of Rocky Patel's recent flourishing is the \"Sun Grown Maduro\". This rich cigar of leaf raised in full sunlight earned 95 points from Cigar Aficionado and was named the magazine's No. 2 Cigar of the Year for 2016 — among the company's highest honours in its pages, and a vivid demonstration that the man who had once hawked his wares through the headwinds of the bust had climbed close to the critical summit. The memory of a cheap, high-volume starting point Patel overwrote completely with a succession of fine limited and standard releases. One pillar running through Rocky Patel's stewardship of the brand is its lineage of anniversary marques marking each milestone: \"Decade\" for the tenth year and the \"Fifteenth Anniversary\" for the fifteenth were followed by the \"Twentieth Anniversary\" and then the 25th-anniversary release. That 25th-anniversary cigar, which Cigar Journal praised with the words \"no half measures\", answered a quarter-century's journey as its summation, with choice aged leaf and precise construction. This practice of condensing each point of attainment into a single cigar at every milestone renders the brand's growth visible and is a deft device for writing its history together with its devotees.\n【Beyond Cigars: The Family and the Master Craftsmen】\nThe Rocky Patel enterprise does not stop at the making of cigars. It has advanced into fine lighters, cutters, ashtrays and other accessories, extending the \"Rocky Patel\" name into a comprehensive brand for the cigar devotee. At its core stand the family and master craftsmen: his brother Nish Patel and cousin Nimish Desai support management and manufacture, and the legendary Cuban-born roller Hamlet Paredes joined as the face of blending and demonstration. Around the company's own factories — TAVICUSA in Estelí and El Paraíso in Danlí — a vertically integrated system runs unbroken from field to rolling table to the world's shops, making possible the union of quality and scale. This formation, built in one generation by an immigrant's son, is one of the success stories of the American cigar industry.\n【Recent Years: Sixty and a Sixtieth Birthday】\nIn 2021 Patel marked his own sixtieth birthday with the release of \"Sixty\". Wearing a lustrous Mexican San Andrés wrapper and using leaf aged over several years before rolling, the cigar then rested a further two years or so before release. Its flavour, into which time had been poured without stint, was richly rewarded: 96 points from Cigar Aficionado and the No. 2 spot as Cigar of the Year for 2022. Looking back, the \"Sun Grown Maduro\" had likewise been named the magazine's No. 2 in 2016 with 95 points; recent years may fairly be called Patel's prime. Rising from nothing as an immigrant's son, abandoning the security of the law, and building the varied succession of Vintage, The Edge, Decade, the anniversary releases and Sixty, Patel has now secured his name as one of the representative entrepreneurs of the modern cigar world. This willingness to lavish time — the most luxurious raw material of all — tells more eloquently than anything how a brand that once competed on volume has transformed itself into a byword for ageing and quality.",
      vitolas: [
        "Vintage 1990 (Honduran broadleaf maduro; the first cigar to bear the RP name)",
        "Vintage 1992 (ten-year-aged Ecuadorian Sumatra wrapper; a long-standing staple)",
        "Vintage 1999 Connecticut (gentle Connecticut Shade)",
        "The Edge Corojo (bold full body in a rough wooden chest)",
        "The Edge Maduro (one of the two original blends of 2004)",
        "Decade (tenth-anniversary release; Ecuadorian Sumatra, 95 points)",
        "Fifteenth Anniversary Torpedo (Ecuadorian Habano wrapper)",
        "Sun Grown Maduro (No. 2 of 2016, 95 points)",
        "Sixty (6 × 60; sixtieth-birthday release, San Andrés wrapper, No. 2 of 2022, 96 points)",
        "Twentieth Anniversary (twentieth-anniversary release)",
        "Royale (a highly rated recent line)",
        "The Edge Habano (Top 25 of 2015, 94 points)"
      ],
      trivia: "【From Lawyer to Cigar Maker】Patel was a Hollywood entertainment lawyer who was captivated by the cigars he smoked with actors between takes, and entered the industry by way of his visits to the Grand Havana Room, the Beverly Hills members' club. Curiously, the club is also where Pete Johnson of Tatuaje worked and discovered cigars.\n【The Riddle of the Indian Name】The old company name Indian Tabac derived not from the founder's Indian origins but from the Indian Motorcycle company, whose name was borrowed in return for a royalty. The Vintage Series carries its ageing years (1990, 1992, 1999 and so on) directly in its marque names."
    },
    "Villiger de Nicaragua": {
      founded: "2021. The building was completed at the end of September, and production began on 13 October",
      founder: "A joint venture between Villiger Söhne of Switzerland (founded 1888; chairman Heinrich Villiger) and Joya de Nicaragua, Nicaragua's oldest premium cigar maker. Day-to-day operations are overseen on the Joya side by Dr Alejandro Martínez Cuenca and Juan Martínez",
      meaning: "\"The Villiger of Nicaragua\". The name states plainly the fact that the Swiss veteran had, for the first time since its founding in 1888, established its own hand-rolling premium factory in Central America. The figure \"1888\" borne by the factory's commemorative marque likewise amounts to a declaration that the founding year had been carried to Nicaragua",
      status: "In operation. Roughly 33,000 square feet (about 1,500 square metres), with a staff of about 100, of whom 80 are rollers; Cigar Journal reported that about sixteen marques were being made there at the time of opening. Standing next door to the Joya de Nicaragua factory, it serves to gather into the company's own plant the hand-rolled Villiger marques previously entrusted to Joya. It is essentially a house factory making the Villiger Group's own brands, not one whose main business is outside contract work",
      history: "【How a Swiss Veteran Came to Own a Central American Factory】\nVilliger Söhne is a cigar company founded in 1888 in Pfeffikon, Switzerland. Long renowned in the European world of dry cigars and machine-made small cigars, it had also entered the field of hand-rolled premium cigars, but their manufacture was handled entirely by commission to other houses' factories: Bock and La Flor de Ynclan entrusted to the ABAM factory in the Dominican Republic, its own factory in Brazil, and in Nicaragua commissions to Joya de Nicaragua. The company had, in other words, engaged with Central America as a \"premium brand without a factory\". What changed this arrangement at its root was Villiger de Nicaragua, the company's own factory, which came on stream in Estelí in 2021. Chairman Heinrich Villiger has said the aim was to create a modern facility producing hand-rolled premium Villiger cigars for the whole world. An article by the Premium Cigar Association reports the judgement that a factory of one's own gives far better control of quality than dependence on several contractors, and allows house brands to be developed with shorter waiting times.\n\n【A Long Association with Joya de Nicaragua】\nThe factory was no solo venture but came into being as a joint enterprise of the Villiger Group and Joya de Nicaragua. The two firms had maintained a contract-manufacturing relationship over many years, and the common explanation among those involved is that it was this accumulated history that enabled Villiger to become a factory owner in Nicaragua. Dr Alejandro Martínez Cuenca and Juan Martínez of Joya de Nicaragua advised on the factory's construction and start-up, and remain in the position of supervising the new plant since it began operating. Villiger thus drew on their manufacturing experience and their knowledge of leaf tobacco. The siting too is symbolic: the new factory was built immediately next door to Joya de Nicaragua's. To build one's own factory beside the very firm to which one had entrusted production, and to have that firm watch over its running — the singularity of this factory lies in its establishing a foothold in Central America not as a competitor but as a joint undertaking.\n\n【Production Begins, 13 October 2021】\nAccording to Cigar Journal, the building was finished at the end of September 2021. The magazine gives the construction period as about twelve months and records that production began on 13 October. The Premium Cigar Association's article, on the other hand, writes that construction \"took nearly two years\"; the figures appear to differ according to where one places the start of works. Either way, a new factory was raised in Central America in the midst of the pandemic. The scale is about 33,000 square feet — in round terms some 1,500 square metres — a medium-sized facility, neither remarkably large nor small. The staff numbers about 100, of whom 80 are rollers, and Villiger indicated an intention to enlarge the workforce in future. Cigar Journal reports that about sixteen marques are made within this space of some 1,500 square metres. The building's design has its own refinements: a new insulating material was adopted for the outer walls, with the aim of raising thermal performance and lowering energy costs — a sensible choice in a cigar factory, where the management of humidity and temperature governs quality. It was on this account that the plant was introduced at the time as \"the most modern cigar factory in Nicaragua\".\n\n【The First Marques Transferred】\nThe factory's first act on starting up was the taking back of the marques that had been entrusted to Joya de Nicaragua. Transferred were Villiger San'Doro (Colorado and Claro), La Vencedora, La Meridiana, Corrida Nicaragua and Casa de Nicaragua. In addition, La Libertad, hitherto made in the Dominican Republic, was moved to Nicaragua on the same occasion. Here lies the essential point: Villiger de Nicaragua is not a factory built merely to create new marques. It was a factory for taking back into the company's own hands the production it had entrusted to others. From the devotee's standpoint, cigars of the same name changed maker at the boundary of 2021.\n\n【1888 Nicaragua — A Cigar for the Factory】\nMade to commemorate the new factory was the \"Villiger 1888 Nicaragua\". 1888 is Villiger's founding year. The composition sets an Ecuadorian Sumatra wrapper over a Nicaraguan binder and a filler of Nicaraguan and Pennsylvania broadleaf, with the Pennsylvania ligero placed as the key to the flavour. The sizes are Coronita 5¾ × 43, Robusto 5 × 50, Toro 6 × 50, Gordo 6 × 60 and Churchill 7 × 50. Its unveiling to the American market came at the 2023 PCA trade show. That a Swiss company should build a factory in Nicaragua and season its commemorative marque with Pennsylvania leaf — the very composition is an apt expression of Villiger's international character.\n\n【The Edición Limitada and the Two Releases of 2025】\nSoon after starting up, the factory began the annual limited series \"Villiger de Nicaragua Edición Limitada\". The 2022 edition came in two shapes, Toro and Torpedo; the 2023 edition was a 6 × 58 perfecto with the same composition as 2022 — Ecuadorian Sumatra wrapper, Mexican binder, Nicaraguan and Pennsylvania fillers — at 17 dollars a cigar, limited to 500 boxes of ten. The 2024 edition, a 7 × 52, was released first in Germany, reaching the United States in early 2025. In this Europe-first sequence one sees the character of a Swiss-owned company. At the 2025 PCA two cigars were announced. One was the \"Villiger 1888 Gran Reserva\": Mexican San Andrés wrapper, Ecuadorian Habano binder, and filler entirely Nicaraguan; the leaf rests at least two years in bales and the rolled cigars a further year at the factory — a limited release in a single 6 × 54 box-pressed Toro, 1,000 boxes of ten, at 22 dollars a cigar. The other was a renewal of \"La Capitana\", its packaging and blend both revised and its manufacture likewise moved to Villiger de Nicaragua — a gentle line of Ecuadorian Connecticut wrapper, Nicaraguan binder and Nicaraguan and Dominican fillers, in boxes of twenty, four shapes, at 9.50 to 11.00 dollars. It exemplifies how owning its own factory allowed even the remaking of existing marques to be handled in-house.\n\n【Its Place in the Villiger Manufacturing Network】\nVilliger does not make its cigars in the Nicaraguan factory alone. It also owns a factory in Brazil, and in the Dominican Republic has Bock and La Flor de Ynclan made under commission at the ABAM factory. Within this network, Villiger de Nicaragua is the company's first Central American factory of its own, positioned as the core of its hand-rolled premium line. Nicaragua has a number of great factories whose main business is contract work, but this factory is not among them. It exists strictly to make Villiger's own marques, and its name almost never arises as a home for outside brands. On the factory map of Estelí it may be called a somewhat singular presence.\n\n【Reservations on Uncertain Points】\nOn the construction period, Cigar Journal gives about twelve months and the Premium Cigar Association \"nearly two years\", and the accounts do not agree. The difference is probably one of how the start of works is defined, but this cannot be settled. As to scale, two figures circulate — \"about 33,000 square feet\" and \"about 1,500 square metres\" — yet 33,000 square feet is about 3,065 square metres, so 1,500 square metres most likely refers to the production floor alone; which extent the original sources intend is not made explicit. The figures of 100 employees and 80 rollers date from the opening, and subsequent staffing increases cannot be confirmed. Neither the equity split of the joint venture nor the name of the local manager serving as head of the factory could be confirmed from published sources. Nor has any source been found that explicitly denies that the factory undertakes any third-party contract manufacture.",
      vitolas: [
        "Villiger 1888 Nicaragua (the core line commemorating the new factory; Ecuadorian Sumatra wrapper with Pennsylvania ligero seasoning the filler; US debut at the 2023 PCA)",
        "Villiger 1888 Gran Reserva (announced at the 2025 PCA; Mexican San Andrés wrapper, 6 × 54 box-pressed Toro, limited to 1,000 boxes of ten, 22 dollars a cigar)",
        "Villiger San'Doro (Colorado / Claro; moved to this factory from commission at Joya de Nicaragua)",
        "Villiger La Vencedora (likewise transferred from Joya)",
        "Villiger La Meridiana (likewise transferred from Joya)",
        "Corrida Nicaragua (likewise transferred from Joya)",
        "Casa de Nicaragua (likewise transferred from Joya)",
        "La Libertad (originally made in the Dominican Republic; moved to Nicaragua when the factory opened)",
        "La Capitana (blend and packaging renewed in 2025, with manufacture moved to this factory; a gentle line in Ecuadorian Connecticut wrapper)",
        "Villiger de Nicaragua Edición Limitada (an annual limited series: Toro/Torpedo in 2022, a 6 × 58 perfecto in 2023, a 7 × 52 in 2024)"
      ],
      trivia: "【Built Next Door】\nVilliger de Nicaragua was built immediately next door to the factory of Joya de Nicaragua, to which it had previously entrusted its manufacture. Supervision since start-up also rests with Dr Alejandro Martínez Cuenca and Juan Martínez on the Joya side.\n【A Cigar Factory Particular about Insulation】\nA new type of insulating material was adopted for the outer walls, with the aim of improving insulation and lowering energy costs — a rational choice in a cigar factory, where the control of temperature and humidity governs quality.\n【A Swiss Founding Year Proclaimed in Nicaragua】\nThe commemorative marque \"1888\" bears Villiger's founding year. While setting a Swiss number upon a Nicaraguan cigar, the key to its flavour was ligero grown in Pennsylvania in the United States.\n【Europe First, America Later】\nThe 2024 edition of the annual Edición Limitada went on sale first in Germany, reaching the United States in early 2025 — an unusual sequence among Nicaraguan factories, most of whose marques originate in the American market."
    },
    "Asylum Cigars": {
      founded: "2012. Debuted that year with the announcement of Asylum 13 at the IPCPR trade show",
      founder: "A three-way venture: founded by Tom Lazuka and Kevin Baxter, joined by Christian Eiroa as partner on the manufacturing side. Lazuka came to cigars from restaurant management at the height of the 1990s cigar boom, and after selling his business moved to the accessories maker Colibri, where he came to know Eiroa. In 2004 he was recruited to Camacho by Eiroa, remaining with the company until Davidoff's purchase of Camacho in 2008",
      meaning: "\"Asylum\" in the sense of a mental institution. The official account of the name is that when Lazuka proposed a cigar of 70 ring gauge — then unprecedented — Eiroa dismissed it as \"the worst idea I've ever heard\" and told him he was insane, whereupon the verdict became the brand name. The number \"13\" of the flagship line plays on ill omen and superstition as part of the brand's imagery, though no primary source stating its origin has been confirmed",
      status: "Active. Distribution and sales of the brand are handled by Christian Eiroa's C.L.E. Cigar Company (of the Fabricas Unidas group), while manufacture is divided between NACSA (Nicaraguan American Cigars S.A.) in Estelí, Nicaragua, and the CLE/Aladino factory in Danlí, Honduras. It is thus a dual-nationality brand with the character of both a \"Nicaraguan marque\" and a \"Honduran marque\", and its new releases of the 2020s in fact lean towards Honduran manufacture",
      history: "【A Plan Called Insane Became the Brand Name】\nAsylum Cigars is a cigar brand begun in 2012, launched by the Americans Tom Lazuka and Kevin Baxter and joined by Christian Eiroa as manufacturing partner. The name Asylum (a mental institution) was born of the verdict passed on the venture itself. What Lazuka brought was a proposal — outlandish for its day — to build the brand around a 70 ring gauge cigar, and on hearing it Eiroa cut it down as \"the worst idea I've ever heard\". He has himself revealed that he decided to make it anyway because he wanted to be able to say \"I told you so\" afterwards. The judgement that the scheme was sheer madness was hoisted up as the shop sign, and this self-mockery was carried on in later line names: Ogre, Schizo, Straight Jacket, Oblongata and Split Personality.\n\n【Three Men Born of the Camacho Network】\nLazuka, a young businessman running restaurants at the time of the 1990s cigar boom, was captivated by cigars. After selling his business he moved to the accessories maker Colibri, where he met Christian Eiroa, then at the head of Camacho Cigars. In 2004 Eiroa brought Lazuka into Camacho as a territory manager, and the two worked at the same company until Camacho was sold to Davidoff in 2008. Eiroa returned to the industry in 2012 with the founding of the C.L.E. Cigar Company, and in the same year joined his old friend Lazuka to launch Asylum. In Eiroa's own division of labour, C.L.E. carried his orthodox blends, while Asylum was the arena for carrying out unbridled notions such as 7 × 70 and 8 × 80.\n\n【The 7 × 70 Gamble】\nLazuka had his reasons for betting on the outsized format. Touring the retail shops in his Camacho and Davidoff years, he had noticed that boxes in the 70 ring gauge class were perpetually sold out on the shelves while the makers' supply utterly failed to keep pace. The problem lay with the factories: moulds of such size scarcely existed in Nicaragua, and the moulds themselves had to be made first. His approach to composing the blend was equally unorthodox: Lazuka first established what leaf existed in quantities sufficient to make a million cigars in the first year, and then composed the best cigar that could be built from that stock. The design was reverse-engineered not from an ideal of taste but from the securing of volume. Debuting at the 2012 IPCPR, Asylum 13 came in 5 × 50, 6 × 60, 7 × 70 and 8 × 80, effectively establishing the outsize category in the trade of the day.\n\n【Production Split between Nicaragua and Honduras】\nAny attempt to describe Asylum by country of origin invariably breeds confusion, for the brand's production has straddled two countries from the very start. The flagship Asylum 13 is a Nicaraguan puro, its maker identified in halfwheel's reviews as NACSA (Nicaraguan American Cigars S.A.) of Estelí. The \"Asylum 13 Corojo\", on the other hand, wrapped in corojo rather than habano, is rolled at Tabacos Rancho Jamastrán in Honduras (Camacho's manufacturing base) with Honduran leaf, its wrapper publicly stated to be corojo grown by Christian's father Julio Eiroa on the family farm. Further, \"Insidious\" with its Ecuadorian Connecticut wrapper, the Honduran puro \"Asylum Nine\" and the 2025 \"Split Personality\" are all made at the CLE factory in Danlí. This CLE/Aladino factory is known as a converted cinema, opened in 1975, once owned by Eiroa's grandfather Antonio Kafati. In short, Asylum is not a brand to be filed away as a \"Nicaraguan boutique marque\": it is a dual-nationality marque in which Nicaraguan and Honduran production run side by side.\n\n【Ever-Expanding Lines and Extreme Ventures】\nAfter its debut Asylum kept expanding at a pace that seemed to turn passing notions directly into products. In 2013 it released the barber-pole \"Asylum 13 Ogre\", entwining candela and habano wrappers, and introduced the brand's first limited edition, \"Straight Jacket\" (a Nicaraguan puro in 5 × 50, 6 × 54, 6 × 60 and 7 × 70, boxes of ten, 8.95 to 11.95 dollars). There followed \"Dragon's Milk\", infused with beer; \"Schizo\", a Cuban-sandwich construction mixing long and short filler; and even the outlandish \"Asylum Nine 90 × 9\", nine inches long by 90 ring gauge. First appearing in 2021, the 90 × 9 became from 2023 an annual fixture timed to April Fools' Day in packaging bearing the animals of the Chinese zodiac; the 2026 edition was in Year of the Horse livery. In June 2025 shipped the banana-flavoured \"Asylum 13 Split Personality\" (40 × 4 at 6.50 dollars, 50 × 5 at 10.50, 54 × 6 at 11.50, 60 × 6 at 12.50, 70 × 7 at 14 dollars), likewise a Honduran puro made at the CLE factory.\n\n【Where the Brand Stands in the 2020s】\nInto 2026 the momentum of new releases shows no slackening. At that year's PCA (April, New Orleans) it was announced that Lazuka's personal blend, hitherto obtainable by no means other than from his own hand, would be commercialised as the \"Asylum Signature Series by Tom Lazuka\": three sizes — 50 × 5 (16 dollars), 46 × 6 (17 dollars) and 54 × 6 (18 dollars) — in boxes of ten, made at the CLE factory. A maduro using Colombian leaf, it is also Asylum's first belicoso maduro. At the same PCA the \"Asylum 13 Arabian Nights\" (five sizes, 9 to 18 dollars) was also announced, but sold in an unusual manner: no samples were handed out at the trade show, and the first outsiders actually to smoke it did so at a launch event held in Marrakesh, Morocco, in late May 2025; the blend remains undisclosed to this day. Both lines began shipping in June 2026. Fourteen years on, the company that made its name on outsize cigars has still not abandoned its habit of trying things first.\n\n【Reservations on Uncertain Points】\n・On the composition of the founders, accounts divide by publication between \"Lazuka and Eiroa, the two of them\" and \"founded by Lazuka and Baxter, later joined by Eiroa\". This entry adopts the latter; the equity split and present holdings are not public.\n・No primary source stating the origin of the number \"13\" has been found. It is most naturally read as folding an unlucky number into the brand's imagery, but there is no confirmation.\n・On the factory making the core Asylum 13, halfwheel's reviews consistently name NACSA (Estelí), while some retail sites state it is made at Tabacos Rancho Jamastrán in Honduras. A change of factory over time cannot be ruled out; this entry gives precedence to halfwheel's account.\n・The ratio of Nicaraguan to Honduran production is not published. The statement that \"the mainstay is NACSA in Nicaragua, the remainder Honduran\" rests on the explanations of retail media, and whether it reflects the reality of the 2020s cannot be confirmed.\n・The figure of one million cigars in the first year rests on the recollections of Christian Eiroa and Lazuka, with no corroboration of actual shipment numbers.\n・The blend of Asylum 13 Arabian Nights is deliberately withheld by the company, so nothing, including its origins, can be recorded.",
      vitolas: [
        "Asylum 13 Seventy (70 × 7) — 7 × 70, 2012 debut. A Nicaraguan puro with habano wrapper; the outsize format that made the brand famous, later joined by a box-pressed version. Made at NACSA in Estelí",
        "Asylum 13 Fifty (50 × 5) — 5 × 50, 2012 debut; the line's standard robusto",
        "Asylum 13 Sixty (60 × 6) — 6 × 60, 2012 debut",
        "Asylum 13 Eighty (80 × 8) — 8 × 80; the largest size, offered from launch and realised only by making new moulds to order",
        "Asylum 13 Ogre — 2013; a barber-pole entwining Nicaraguan candela and habano wrappers, chiefly in large sizes led by the 7 × 70",
        "Asylum 13 Corojo — a blend of Honduran leaf, its wrapper corojo grown on Julio Eiroa's own farm; made at Tabacos Rancho Jamastrán in Honduras",
        "Straight Jacket — 5 × 50 / 6 × 54 / 6 × 60 / 7 × 70; the brand's first limited edition, a Nicaraguan puro in boxes of ten at 8.95 to 11.95 dollars a cigar",
        "Insidious — Ecuadorian Connecticut wrapper over Honduran binder and filler; made at the CLE factory in Danlí, with a sweet-tipped derivative",
        "Asylum Nine 90 × 9 (April Fools' 90 × 9) — nine inches by 90 ring gauge; a Honduran puro from the CLE factory. First seen in 2021, and since 2023 an annual April Fools' limited edition in Chinese-zodiac packaging (the 2026 edition in Year of the Horse livery)",
        "Asylum 13 Split Personality — 40 × 4 (6.50 dollars) / 50 × 5 (10.50) / 54 × 6 (11.50) / 60 × 6 (12.50) / 70 × 7 (14 dollars); shipped June 2025. A banana-flavoured Honduran puro made at the CLE factory",
        "Asylum Signature Series by Tom Lazuka — 50 × 5 (16 dollars) / 46 × 6 (17 dollars) / 54 × 6 (18 dollars), boxes of ten; shipped late May 2026. CLE-made maduro including Colombian leaf, the company's first belicoso maduro",
        "Asylum 13 Arabian Nights — five sizes, 9 to 18 dollars a cigar; unveiled in Marrakesh, Morocco, at the end of May 2026 and shipped in June. The blend is undisclosed"
      ],
      trivia: "【A Name Born of an Insult】Christian Eiroa's reaction on hearing the 70 ring gauge proposal was \"the worst idea I've ever heard\". Having had his sanity questioned, Lazuka turned the charge around and named the brand \"Asylum\". Eiroa has said he agreed to make it in the end because he wanted to be able to say \"I told you so\" if it failed.\n【Volume Decided before Taste】Lazuka first established which leaf could yield a million cigars in the first year, then built the best blend that leaf allowed — a design in reverse order, the very opposite of the usual blending philosophy of working backwards from an ideal taste.\n【No Moulds Existed】To make a 7 × 70 at all, no moulds of such size existed in Nicaragua's factories at the time, and work had to begin with making new ones.\n【The Factory Is a Former Cinema】The CLE/Aladino factory, the manufacturing base on the Honduran side, is a converted cinema opened in 1975 and once owned by Christian Eiroa's grandfather Antonio Kafati.\n【The April Fools' 90 × 9】The off-the-scale nine-inch, 90 ring gauge cigar has been released almost every year since its 2021 appearance to coincide with 1 April. Since 2023 packaging bearing the Chinese zodiac has become the custom; the 2026 edition was in Year of the Horse livery.\n【First Lit in Marrakesh】The 2026 Arabian Nights handed out no samples to retailers at the PCA trade show; the launch event in Marrakesh, Morocco, became the first place in the world it was smoked. The blend is still undisclosed."
    }
  };
  BRANDS_DATA.nicaragua.forEach(function (b) {
    var e = EN[b.en];
    if (!e) return;
    if (e.vitolas && b.vitolas && e.vitolas.length !== b.vitolas.length) delete e.vitolas;
    Object.assign(b, e);
  });
})();
